<!-- ==================== PAGE 7: 2. LITERATURE REVIEW ==================== -->
<br clear="all" style="page-break-before:always" />

<h1>2. LITERATURE REVIEW AND FEASIBILITY STUDY</h1>

<h2>2.1 Review of Existing Systems and Related Work</h2>
<p>
    An investigation of current public and private platforms demonstrates noticeable structural deficiencies:
</p>
<p class="noindent">
    1. <strong>JanSamarth National Portal:</strong> JanSamarth connects applicants to multiple credit schemes; however, its UI is heavily bureaucratic and requires mandatory multi-level registrations before basic scheme discovery. It lacks working capital simulation and automatic DPR synthesis.<br>
    2. <strong>Udyam Registration Portal:</strong> Provides official enterprise identity certificates but offers no financial advisory, scheme filtering, or bank credit documentation.<br>
    3. <strong>Commercial Bookkeeping Apps (Vyapar, Khatabook):</strong> Excel in simple merchant ledger management but do not connect users to government subsidies or generate bank loan appraisal reports.<br>
    4. <strong>Unorganised Consultants:</strong> Impose substantial financial burdens (₹5,000.00 to ₹15,000.00) on impoverished applicants seeking micro-credit.
</p>

<p class="table-caption">Table 2.1: Feature Comparison Matrix of Existing Platforms vs Samarth Business</p>
